From d03bf9d935d043f809fd48428f146e19dd169c79 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Micheal Waltz <micheal@ecliptik.com>
Date: Sat, 19 Sep 2026 00:06:41 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] DOS-PORT: patch 0119 -- un-inline FramesEqualFast

One attribute, +1.20 fps on bench8 anim12 at Mode 13h: 38.92 (baseline
ensemble of four) to 40.12, with two layout variants returning the
IDENTICAL 40.12 -- tighter than the 0.19 layout floor would predict.

Why it works, as far as we can tell. This render path behaves as though it
is bound by instruction-cache footprint rather than by instructions
retired, on a 486DX2-66 with an 8 KB unified cache. Three separate small
additions of EXECUTED code to it each cost about a frame (patch 0104
-1.21, a dirty-bbox latch -1.05, repaint counters -1.07) while
size-matched code that is emitted but NEVER EXECUTED costs only -0.23. So
the cost tracks what occupies cache lines at run time, not what the code
does -- and removing a large body from the hot function should therefore
gain about a frame, which is what this measures.

That is a hypothesis fitting seven observations, not a proven mechanism:
a 486 gives us no cache-miss counter. It is recorded as the reason this
patch exists, not as a claim.

Note what this recovers rather than wins: 40.12 is the same figure patch
0103 measured before 0104 existed. This gives back the ground 0104 took,
whose own mechanism remains unexplained after five dead hypotheses.

Screening note for future candidates: verify a function is actually
inlined in the baseline before spending cells on un-inlining it. A second
candidate here, TryFastIntegerNearestBlit, was already out-of-line (2
symbols in the baseline binary), so the attribute changed nothing and its
null result is no evidence either way.
---
 Engine/gfx/ali3dsw.cpp | 2 +-
 1 file changed, 1 insertion(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/Engine/gfx/ali3dsw.cpp b/Engine/gfx/ali3dsw.cpp
index 88237d841..20561a36d 100644
--- a/Engine/gfx/ali3dsw.cpp
+++ b/Engine/gfx/ali3dsw.cpp
@@ -967,7 +967,7 @@ static bool TryFastIntegerNearestBlit(SDL_Surface *src, SDL_Surface *dst, const
 // x86 tolerates unaligned reads (just potentially slower, never
 // incorrect), so no alignment precondition is required for
 // correctness, only for best-case speed.
-static bool FramesEqualFast(const void *a, const void *b, size_t n)
+static __attribute__((noinline)) bool FramesEqualFast(const void *a, const void *b, size_t n)
 {
     const size_t nwords = n / 4;
     const Uint32 *wa = (const Uint32 *)a;
